# Rocdown report authoring: friction and missing features

**Exploratory use-case evaluation — 17 August 2026**

This report evaluates Rocdown as the source format for a substantial research
and design report. It is based on converting and validating the 673-line
[Rocci branding and community foundation
report](/branding-and-community-foundation/), including seven local image
assets, page metadata, tables, code, long-form navigation, and a static Rocs
build.

It distinguishes shipped behavior from recommendations. It does not approve
new syntax or change Rocdown's Markdown-first boundary.

The `@docs` / `@img` spellings in this report are historical (17 August 2026).
The public language is now `:name[params]`; leftover `@docs` / `@img` are
removal errors.

:note[title: "Bottom line"] {{
    Rocdown is already a strong source format for the prose layer of a report.
    The friction is concentrated at the document-product layer: figures,
    citations, report metadata, asset portability, print/export, and semantic
    parity between standalone Rocdown and Rocs.
}}

## Executive assessment

The exercise was successful, but not yet frictionless.

The source remained readable, almost all ordinary Markdown copied across
unchanged, `@page` gave the report a title and description, and native `@img`
declarations made image intent more explicit than Markdown image shorthand.
The parser recognized all seven images. A zero-configuration Rocs check
succeeded, and a static build produced hashed assets, semantic HTML, an
automatic heading outline, strict CSP, and basic print rules.

The problems appeared when the same source crossed renderer boundaries:

- standalone Rocdown accepts image sizing, class, loading, and decoding fields,
  but its documented single-file runtime has no asset pipeline;
- Rocs copies and hashes the assets, but currently projects `@img` into the
  simpler Markdown image node and loses `width`, `height`, `class`, `loading`,
  and `decoding`;
- `@page.theme: "paper"` controls the standalone shell, while the Rocs build
  uses the Rocs site theme and generic documentation chrome;
- the static output therefore used “Documentation” navigation and the current
  placeholder `r` even though the report explicitly says that mark must not be
  a design starting point;
- the document had to use an unnumbered Sources section because ordinary
  Rocdown leaves footnotes disabled;
- image captions, credits, figure numbering, cross-references, a report cover,
  and print-quality pagination all require manual prose, consumer-specific
  components, or custom CSS.

The right response is not to turn Rocdown into a word processor. It is to define
a small, portable static-document contract, then let a Rocs report presentation
own report chrome and export.

## The exercised workflow

The branding-report task required:

1. long-form prose with four heading levels;
2. an executive recommendation, qualifications, and source list;
3. several comparison and palette tables;
4. fenced architecture text and inline code;
5. seven local visual concept sheets with meaningful alternative text;
6. a dated exploratory status;
7. browser-readable output with navigation;
8. durable static assets;
9. a report document that can later be shared or printed;
10. a canonical source link from the knowledge bundle.

The actual workflow was:

```text
Markdown report
  → copy to .rocdown
  → add @page metadata
  → replace seven Markdown images with @img blocks
  → inspect the Rocdown AST
  → run Rocs knowledge validation
  → run Rocs check on the report directory
  → build a temporary static site and inspect its HTML
```

The content conversion itself was easy. Most friction arose after parsing,
when deciding which consumer defines image semantics, assets, layout, and
export.

## What already fits well

### Markdown-first authoring

The most important design choice held up. Headings, paragraphs, lists,
blockquotes, links, tables, emphasis, code spans, and fences needed no
translation. The source remains understandable in an editor without a preview
and degrades sensibly in generic Markdown-oriented tools.

This is the core advantage Rocdown should protect. Report features should remain
bounded declarations around Markdown rather than introduce a second prose
grammar.

### Explicit page metadata

`@page` is clearer than hidden filename conventions for title, description,
route, theme, and draft state. Rocs also falls back from metadata to the first
heading, which makes a minimal report pleasant to start.

The metadata record can carry arbitrary Roc fields for a custom standalone
layout. That is a useful extension point, even though the default shell and Rocs
currently extract only a limited visible subset.

### Static validation and inspectability

`rocci inspect --ast` exposed the native image nodes and page controls without
running a server. `rocs check` validated the whole report directory without
writing output. This separation is excellent for agent-authored and CI-authored
documents.

The LSP already knows `@img` and `@docs figure` fields. Missing editor
intelligence is not the primary blocker for this use case.

### Rocs assets and outline

Rocs found the local `assets/` directory, content-hashed every referenced
concept sheet, rewrote the URLs, and produced a heading outline automatically.
Those are exactly the capabilities a publishable HTML report needs.

The site build also preserved the report title, description, headings,
alternative text, tables, code, and source links in semantic HTML.

### Safe defaults

Raw Markdown HTML remains disabled, fenced code stays inert, and Rocs emits a
strict static CSP. A report system should keep these properties. Rich report
features do not justify allowing arbitrary authored HTML by default.

## Friction and missing-feature matrix

| Area | Current behavior | Report consequence | Priority |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Static semantic parity | Rocdown and Rocs use different projections for some declarations | One source renders differently by consumer | P0 |
| `@img` fidelity | Standalone retains all fields; Rocs retains only source, alt, and title | Loading, decoding, size, and class silently disappear | P0 |
| Local assets | Rocs hashes assets; standalone Rocdown documents assets as unimplemented | A single-file preview cannot reliably show the same local figures | P0 |
| Internal page links | Rocs validates relative `.rocdown` targets but can emit the source URL unchanged | A link passes checks and is broken in static output | P0 |
| Citations | Ordinary Rocdown footnotes are disabled | Research claims need manual source lists and no backlinks | P0 |
| Figure semantics | `@img` is bare; `@docs figure` supplies captions but duplicates and then ignores its required alt field | Captions, credits, and accessibility are awkward and error-prone | P0 |
| One-file rendering | `rocci build` emits Roc; `rocs build` targets a site root | No obvious “render this report” command | P0 |
| Presentation selection | `@page.theme` affects standalone output but not Rocs site chrome | Paper in one renderer becomes Documentation in another | P1 |
| Report metadata | Author, date, status, revision, and license have no standard visible model | Metadata becomes ad hoc bold prose | P1 |
| Report layout | No report profile or title-page shell | Generic docs navigation surrounds a standalone report | P1 |
| Print and PDF | Rocs has basic print hiding; Paper has no report pagination contract | No controlled cover, running header, page numbers, or break behavior | P1 |
| Long-document TOC | Rocs supplies a screen outline; ordinary Rocdown has no automatic TOC token | Standalone and printed reports lack a generated contents section | P1 |
| Wide tables | Renderers emit a bare table without a scrolling wrapper | Four-column comparison tables are fragile on narrow screens | P1 |
| Cross-references | Heading links exist; figures and tables have no labels or numbering | “See Figure 3” is manual and can drift | P2 |
| Responsive media | No `srcset`, `sizes`, art direction, or image variants | Large research images are downloaded everywhere | P2 |
| Reuse and splitting | Rocs has `@docs include`; standalone Rocdown does not | Large reports cannot be split portably | P2 |
| Formatting | No formatter is shipped | Long generated declarations and metadata can drift stylistically | P2 |
| Rich figure groups | One image per figure; no gallery or comparison plate | Visual evaluations need custom components or repetitive headings | P2 |
| Office export | HTML is available; PDF and DOCX are not first-class outputs | External review may require a separate generation pipeline | P3 |

## Detailed findings

### 1. The shared static-document contract is incomplete

Rocdown has one parsed document, but consumers do not yet preserve the same
static semantics.

The standalone lowerer turns `@img` into an HTML image with:

- `src` and `alt`;
- optional `title`;
- optional `width` and `height`;
- optional custom class;
- optional `loading` and `decoding`.

Rocs currently converts that declaration to `MdNode::Image`, whose model
contains only URL, title, alt, and span. The static branding-report build
therefore produced correct hashed URLs and alternative text but omitted the
authored `loading="lazy"` and `decoding="async"` on all seven images. The same
projection necessarily drops width, height, and custom class.

This is more serious than a missing convenience: it violates the expectation
that a static declaration means the same thing in both supported consumers.

**Recommendation:** introduce one shared rich static-image representation or a
lossless projection contract. Rocs should rewrite asset URLs without narrowing
the image fields. Add consumer-parity snapshot tests using the same source.

### 2. The three image idioms overlap without one clear path

Rocdown currently offers three related forms:

1. Markdown image syntax for the lowest-friction image;
2. `@img` for explicit attributes;
3. `@docs figure` for a semantic wrapper, caption, and credit.

That can be a sensible layering, but the boundaries are not coherent today.
`@docs figure` requires its own `alt` field while its body must contain exactly
one image. The renderers use the nested image's alt text, not the figure field.
An author can therefore satisfy validation with a figure-level alt while still
emitting an empty-alt image.

The branding report avoided `@docs figure` and placed prose after each `@img`.
That kept the source portable, but it lost semantic `figure` and `figcaption`
markup.

**Recommended near-term model:**

- keep Markdown images as shorthand;
- keep `@img` as the canonical image element;
- make meaningful images require `alt`, with an explicit `decorative:
  Bool.true` escape hatch rather than silently defaulting to empty alt;
- make `@docs figure` portable across standalone and Rocs;
- remove its redundant alt field and require the nested image to own alt;
- preserve caption and credit as figure semantics in every renderer.

A new `@figure` convenience declaration may eventually be cleaner, but it
should not be added until the existing image and docs-component models share
one AST contract.

### 3. Image sizing is underspecified

The current reference treats values such as `"50px"` and `"100%"` as valid
`width` strings and lowers them into HTML `width` attributes. This mixes
intrinsic image dimensions with CSS display sizing.

A report needs both:

- intrinsic pixel width and height to reserve layout space and reduce shift;
- responsive display constraints such as full measure, max width, or a
  two-column plate.

**Recommendation:** separate numeric intrinsic dimensions from display
presentation. Keep CSS choices in a class, figure layout, or typed presentation
field. Validate `loading` and `decoding` enumerations rather than accepting any
string literal.

### 4. Local assets are not portable between preview modes

Rocs provides the right publication behavior: asset discovery, validation,
hashing, copying, and URL rewriting. Standalone Rocdown explicitly lists assets
as unimplemented. As a result, the most direct command for a single
`.rocdown` file is not the most reliable command for previewing a report with
local images.

Authors should not have to learn that “single document” and “static report”
select different asset semantics.

**Recommendation:** define a shared source-relative asset resolver. The
standalone preview can serve assets without hashing; Rocs can hash the same
resolved asset at publication time. Both paths should diagnose missing files at
the source declaration.

### 5. Validated page links can remain unrewritten

The first version of this evaluation linked to the sibling branding source as
`BRANDING_AND_COMMUNITY_REPORT.rocdown`. `rocs check` accepted the target and
the catalog resolved it, but the static HTML retained that literal href instead
of rewriting it to `/branding-and-community-foundation/`. The emitted site does not
copy Rocdown source files, so the validated link was broken.

This is the link equivalent of the image-field problem: validation and output
do not consume one resolved semantic model.

**Recommendation:** rewrite internal page and heading links to resolved routes
before static article rendering. Add a build-level test for relative Rocdown
links, explicit routes, derived routes, aliases, and fragments. A passing link
check must guarantee a usable emitted href.

### 6. Research citations are a first-order gap

Footnotes are already represented in the Rocdown Markdown AST and used by the
isolated OKF body profile, but ordinary Rocdown leaves them disabled. This is
noticeable in any evidence-based report: the author must use inline links or a
manual Sources section without keyed references and backlinks.

**Recommendation:** enable footnotes in ordinary static Rocdown, or provide an
equally lightweight citation declaration that lowers to accessible footnotes.
Do not require Roc code for citations. Bibliography formatting and external
citation databases can remain later work.

This should be treated as P0 for “report” readiness even though it is not P0 for
general web-page authoring.

### 7. `@page.meta` is extensible but not a report metadata contract

The branding report expressed date and authority as bold prose. Although the
`meta` record can contain more fields, the default shell only uses title for
the document title, and Rocs visibly extracts title and description. There is
no standard presentation for:

- author or organization;
- publication and revision dates;
- lifecycle state such as draft or exploratory;
- license;
- document version;
- confidentiality or audience;
- canonical repository revision.

A custom Roc layout can interpret arbitrary fields, but requiring a layout
module turns a simple report into application development.

**Recommendation:** define a small optional report metadata vocabulary under
`@page.meta` and expose it to Rocs presentation without making those fields
mandatory for ordinary pages.

### 8. A report needs a presentation profile, not documentation-site chrome

A zero-configuration Rocs build was impressively functional, but it wrapped the
report in the default Documentation site:

- “Documentation” became the site and navigation label;
- sidebar, breadcrumb, and next/previous infrastructure were created for one
  report;
- the current placeholder `r` appeared in the header;
- `@page.theme: "paper"` did not select the Paper presentation.

This is reasonable for a documentation generator and wrong for a standalone
report.

**Recommendation:** add a Rocs-owned report presentation, selected explicitly
through configuration or a statically extracted page metadata value. It should:

- display title, subtitle, status, date, author, and revision;
- omit site navigation by default;
- include an optional screen outline and contents section;
- use the neutral Paper family unless overridden;
- carry no project logo unless configured;
- support a cover mode without forcing a separate source format.

Rocs should continue to own the static shell. Rocdown only needs to expose
portable metadata and semantic content.

### 9. Print is present but not report-grade

Rocs already hides the main site chrome in print, which is a good foundation.
The default Paper theme has no print-specific contract, and neither path
provides report pagination features such as:

- title-page behavior;
- page size and margins;
- running title or revision;
- page numbering;
- keep-with-next for headings;
- break avoidance for figures, tables, and code;
- widows and orphans;
- printed link destinations;
- deterministic light-color output.

**Recommendation:** first make browser printing reliable, then add a
deterministic `rocs render --pdf` or equivalent export. PDF is higher priority
than DOCX because it can preserve the HTML/CSS rendering model. DOCX should be
treated as an optional interoperability exporter, not as the source of truth.

### 10. Tables need a responsive and printable container

The source experience for GFM tables is good. The output emits a bare table.
Long comparison tables need an overflow container on small screens and careful
page behavior in print. Applying overflow to the table itself is not a robust
substitute for a wrapper.

**Recommendation:** render tables through a stable `.rd-table-container` with
horizontal scrolling on screen and explicit print fallback. Preserve semantic
table markup inside it. Table captions and column alignment can follow later.

### 11. Navigation exists, but a document TOC does not

Rocs uses extracted headings to build an “On this page” outline. That solves
screen navigation inside the documentation shell. The standalone Paper
renderer has no equivalent, and the outline disappears in print.

**Recommendation:** expose the extracted heading outline as a portable static
value and support an explicit contents insertion point. Avoid a magic Markdown
token if a bounded declaration such as `@docs contents` fits the existing
model. The same heading IDs should drive screen outline, contents, and
cross-reference validation.

### 12. Long-report maintenance needs formatting and reuse

At 673 lines, consistent declaration formatting and section reuse matter.
Rocs already has safe, cycle-checked `@docs include` behavior, while standalone
Rocdown does not. Rocdown also has no formatter.

**Recommendation:** move only the safe static-fragment include contract into a
shared layer if standalone parity is a goal. Add a formatter before adding many
more field-based declarations. Keep generated reports stable under repeated
formatting and preserve source spans for diagnostics.

## Recommended capability architecture

The cleanest separation is:

```text
Rocdown core
  Markdown + page metadata + static semantic nodes
  image / figure / footnote / table / headings
  shared validation and source-relative asset references
                 │
                 ├── standalone Rocdown shell
                 │     fast local preview, Paper defaults
                 │
                 └── Rocs static presentations
                       documentation site
                       standalone report
                       future publication profiles
```

This keeps the existing ownership boundary intact:

- Rocdown owns the authored semantic contract.
- Rocs owns static catalog work, asset publication, outlines, and report chrome.
- Rocci themes own visible shells.
- Exporters consume the same static model rather than reparsing or narrowing it.

The anti-pattern to avoid is teaching each consumer how to reinterpret
`@img`, `@docs figure`, metadata, and footnotes independently.

## Prioritized implementation plan

### P0 — make one source trustworthy

1. Preserve every `@img` field through the Rocs static pipeline.
2. Add parity tests comparing standalone and Rocs image semantics.
3. Rewrite validated internal page links to their emitted routes.
4. Correct `@docs figure` accessibility: nested `@img.alt` is authoritative;
   remove or apply the currently required figure-level alt.
5. Require alt for meaningful native images and add an explicit decorative
   form.
6. Enable ordinary static footnotes with accessible backlinks.
7. Resolve and diagnose source-relative local assets in standalone preview.
8. Add a one-file static render command or an obvious single-report mode that
   requires no hand-written site configuration.

### P1 — make the result feel like a report

1. Define optional report metadata under `@page.meta`.
2. Add a Rocs report presentation with no default logo or documentation chrome.
3. Unify or clearly define theme selection across standalone and Rocs.
4. Add a generated contents insertion point backed by the existing heading
   model.
5. Add responsive table wrappers and portable figure/caption styling.
6. Add report-grade print CSS and deterministic PDF export.
7. Separate numeric intrinsic image dimensions from responsive display sizing.

### P2 — make large reports maintainable

1. Add figure and table labels, numbering, and checked cross-references.
2. Add responsive image sources and visual comparison groups.
3. Share safe static includes where consumer parity is intended.
4. Ship canonical Rocdown formatting and declaration snippets.
5. Add lint rules for missing metadata, duplicate captions, unreferenced
   figures, oversized assets, and invalid image option values.

### P3 — interoperability and publishing depth

1. Consider bibliography-file integration after footnotes are stable.
2. Consider DOCX export only as a derived artifact.
3. Consider editorial annotations, comments, and review states outside the core
   prose grammar.
4. Consider multi-report collections only through Rocs rather than adding
   collection semantics to single-file Rocdown.

## Acceptance scenarios

A “frictionless report” milestone should be demonstrated by executable
scenarios rather than a feature checklist.

### Scenario A — first report

Given one `report.rocdown` and an adjacent `assets/` directory, one documented
command produces a browser-ready report. No `rocs.toml`, Roc layout module, or
manual asset copy is required.

### Scenario B — renderer parity

The same figure source yields equivalent semantic HTML through standalone
Rocdown and Rocs. Asset URLs may differ after hashing, but alt, caption, credit,
dimensions, class, loading, and decoding do not disappear.

### Scenario C — accessible image intent

A meaningful image without alt fails with a focused diagnostic. A decorative
image requires explicit intent and emits empty alt. A figure caption does not
substitute for image alternative text.

### Scenario D — checked internal links

A relative Rocdown source link, explicit route, derived route, alias, and
heading fragment all emit usable static hrefs. Any link that passes validation
is guaranteed to target a generated page or copied asset.

### Scenario E — research citations

Keyed footnotes render with forward links and backlinks in both preview modes.
Missing definitions and duplicate labels produce diagnostics.

### Scenario F — report presentation

The report view shows title, status, date, author, and revision; it contains no
generic Documentation header or placeholder logo. Screen outline and printable
contents use the same extracted headings.

### Scenario G — responsive and printed output

At narrow width, wide tables and code remain reachable without page-level
horizontal overflow. In print or PDF, headings stay with following content,
figures and tables avoid accidental splits where possible, URLs remain
recoverable, and the cover and page numbering are deterministic.

### Scenario H — round-trip maintenance

Formatting the report twice produces no second diff. `rocs check` validates it
without writing output. A missing asset, invalid image option, broken internal
link, or malformed footnote fails before publication.

## Non-goals

To preserve Rocdown's character, this work should not:

- introduce arbitrary raw HTML as the normal escape hatch;
- make WYSIWYG editing a compiler-core responsibility;
- replicate a full desktop publishing system;
- put site navigation or asset hashing into the Rocdown parser;
- make PDF or DOCX the canonical document model;
- require every ordinary Rocdown page to carry report metadata;
- add JavaScript merely to make static reports navigable;
- collapse the OKF knowledge profile into executable Rocdown.

## Further investigations

The following questions deserve focused prototypes before syntax is approved:

1. **Image AST design:** preserve rich fields directly or normalize Markdown
   image, `@img`, and figures into a shared media node.
2. **Figure syntax:** repair `@docs figure` or introduce a smaller `@figure`
   form after comparing source readability.
3. **Report selection:** use `meta.kind`, a Rocs configuration profile, or a
   dedicated command flag without creating conflicting controls.
4. **Footnote default:** enable globally or behind a page/profile option after
   testing compatibility with existing documents.
5. **Static asset root:** define whether paths are source-relative,
   content-root-relative, or explicitly prefixed, and make diagnostics explain
   the choice.
6. **PDF engine:** compare browser print output with a dedicated paged-media
   tool while keeping HTML as the primary render.
7. **Performance:** measure large images, syntax-highlighted code, and
   hundred-page reports before adding responsive variants or collection
   features.
8. **Interoperability:** test how generic Markdown tools preserve unknown
   Rocdown declarations and whether a derived plain-Markdown export is useful.

## Sources and evidence

This evaluation used current repository behavior on 17 August 2026:

- `reports/branding/BRANDING_AND_COMMUNITY_REPORT.rocdown` — the exercised
  long-form source.
- `crates/rocci-rocdown/README.md` — implemented language and deferred-feature
  contract.
- `crates/rocci-rocdown/src/img.rs` — native image field extraction.
- `crates/rocci-rocdown/src/lower.rs` — standalone image and Markdown lowering.
- `crates/rocci-rocdown/src/ast.rs` — current Markdown image model and footnote
  nodes.
- `crates/rocs/src/docs.rs` — Rocs declaration projection, figure validation,
  includes, and static components.
- `crates/rocs/src/article.rs` — static HTML rendering.
- `crates/rocs/src/plan.rs` — asset hashing, URL rewriting, outline, and
  generated view data.
- `crates/rocci-theme/src/themes/chrome.css` and `paper.css` — standalone
  reading presentation.
- `crates/rocs/templates/RocsTheme.rocci` and `DocsComponents.rocci` — Rocs
  screen, print, outline, and docs-component presentation.
- `docs/reference/rocdown.rocdown`, `docs/reference/rocs.rocdown`, and
  `docs/reference/cli.rocdown` — current public documentation.

Observed commands:

```sh
cargo run -q -p rocci-cli -- inspect --ast \
  reports/branding/BRANDING_AND_COMMUNITY_REPORT.rocdown

cargo run -q -p rocs-cli -- check reports/branding --format terminal

cargo run -q -p rocs-cli -- build reports/branding --output TEMP/site
```

The static build completed successfully and emitted all seven hashed visual
assets. Inspection of its HTML confirmed the useful outline, the loss of the
authored loading and decoding attributes, and the unrevised relative Rocdown
href described above.
